          "title": "Thermodynamics of Vancomycin Binding to D-Ala-D-Ala vs D-Ala-D-Lac Mutants",
          "difficulty": "Advanced",
          "statement": "The glycopeptide antibiotic vancomycin binds to the peptidoglycan cell wall precursor terminal peptide $-L\\text{-Lys}-D\\text{-Ala}-D\\text{-Ala}$ through a network of 5 cooperative hydrogen bonds. The association constant at $298\\text{ K}$ is $K_a = 1.0\\times 10^6\\text{ M}^{-1}$.\n(a) Compute the standard Gibbs free energy of binding $\\Delta G^\\circ_{\\text{bind}}$ for vancomycin to $-D\\text{-Ala}-D\\text{-Ala}$.\n(b) In vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE), the terminal dipeptide is modified by the VanA ligase to $-D\\text{-Ala}-D\\text{-Lac}$ (ester linkage instead of amide linkage). This single substitution replaces one $\\text{N}-\\text{H}\\cdots\\text{O}=\\text{C}$ hydrogen bond with an oxygen-oxygen lone pair repulsion ($\\text{O}\\cdots\\text{O}=\\text{C}$). The binding constant plummets to $K_a = 1.0\\times 10^3\\text{ M}^{-1}$.\nCalculate the thermodynamic destabilization $\\Delta(\\Delta G^\\circ)$ caused by this single atom replacement, and explain why this 1,000-fold drop completely abolishes antibiotic efficacy.",
